.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt READ 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm read ,
.Nm readv ,
.Nm pread ,
.Nm pread64 ,
.Nm preadv
.Nd read bytes from a descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn read "int fd" "void *buf" "size_t count"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn pread "int fd" "void *buf" "size_t count" "off_t offset"
.In sys/uio.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn readv "int fd" "const struct iovec *iov" "int iovcnt"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn preadv "int fd" "const struct iovec *iov" "int iovcnt" "off_t offset"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn read
reads up to
.Fa count
bytes from
.Fa fd
into
.Fa buf .
A successful call may return fewer bytes than requested; a count past
0x7ffff000, Linux's
.Dv MAX_RW_COUNT ,
reads at most that many, so that the count returned is never negative.
The descriptor is checked first, then the range with the original count:
an overflowing range or one past the top of user memory is
.Er EFAULT .
On the i686 kernel, a count negative as the kernel's signed size is
.Er EINVAL
after that range check, before the transfer is limited.
An i386 call on x86-64 uses the kernel's 64-bit signed size for this check.
Then a read at the open file description's offset whose end would pass
the signed 64-bit range is
.Er EINVAL ,
as Linux's rw_verify_area has it, with the original count; a stream,
which has no offset, a directory, and
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /mem
and
.Pa /dev/mem ,
whose offsets are unsigned, are never refused for it.
A zero-length read checks the descriptor as a longer one does, that it
exists, is open for reading, is open on an object with a read method and
is no directory, and that the buffer does not pass the top of user
memory, then returns zero without touching the buffer;
an eventfd and a timerfd, which read whole eight-byte counts, refuse it
with
.Er EINVAL ,
as Linux does, while
.Fn readv
of zero bytes returns zero for them, as Linux's does.
.Pp
Regular files are read at the open file description's offset.
A positive result advances that offset by the number of bytes read.
A file of a mounted file system is read up to the first page of the
buffer that cannot be written, and the bytes before it are returned, as
Linux's copy stops at its first fault; a buffer whose first page cannot
be written gives
.Er EFAULT
only when the file has a byte to read at the offset, and zero when it
has none, as on Linux; the file's read decides, so an mqueue file, whose
size is its queue's bytes, ends where its status line does.
Descriptors shared through duplication or fork share the offset.
Directories cannot be read with these calls: a read of any count returns
.Er EISDIR ,
after
.Er EFAULT
for a buffer that passes the top of user memory, of no bytes too, as on
Linux.
Use
.Xr getdents64 2
to read directory entries.
.Pp
A pipe read returns the available bytes, up to the requested count.
An empty pipe waits while a writer remains open and returns zero after
the last writer closes.
The console and pseudoterminal slaves use the terminal line discipline:
canonical input waits for a line; raw input follows the
.Dv VMIN
setting.
A pseudoterminal master reads output from the slave and returns zero
after all slaves close and the buffered output is drained.
Closing the master wakes every blocked slave reader.
The slave drains queued input, then returns zero on every read, including
nonblocking reads.
This remains true when
.Dv SIGHUP
is ignored or blocked.
.Pp
A background process reading its controlling terminal receives
.Dv SIGTTIN .
If that signal is blocked or ignored, or the process group is orphaned,
the read fails with
.Er EIO .
A read of
.Pa /fb
returns one ownership event, waiting interruptibly unless O_NONBLOCK
is set, and returns EOF after the owner releases it and its events drain.
See
.Xr console 4 .
.Pp
A stream socket returns buffered bytes and returns zero after the peer's
FIN once its buffered data is exhausted.
A datagram read consumes one message and discards any bytes beyond the
buffer capacity.
A zero-byte datagram returns zero without indicating end of input.
Protocol-bearing raw sockets can also be read; an ioctl-only socket
returns
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Pp
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
makes an empty pipe, terminal, pseudoterminal, or socket return
.Er EAGAIN
instead of waiting, unless it is at end of input; a read that would not
wait, an empty noncanonical terminal with
.Dv VMIN
zero or a console read by the framebuffer owner, returns zero.
A signal can interrupt a wait with
.Er EINTR .
Files under
.Pa /proc
read from the snapshot made when opened, except
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /oom_score_adj ,
whose text each read makes, and
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /mem ,
which reads the memory as it is then.
A read of one holds the open file description for its length, as
.Xr lseek 2
holds it to move the offset, so a seek through a descriptor that shares
it waits for the read; so does a read of
.Pa /dev/bootlog
or
.Pa /dev/screenlog ,
and a second read through the description waits for the first.
Other devices use their own read operation.
Mouse reads return whole packets without waiting and return zero when
the buffer cannot hold one packet.
.Ss Scatter reads
.Fn readv
reads into the
.Fa iovcnt
buffers described by
.Fa iov :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct iovec {
        void *iov_base;
        size_t iov_len;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Buffers are filled in order; zero-length entries are skipped.
The count must be between 0 and 1024, and no length may be negative as a
signed size, which for a 32-bit program is one past 2147483647.
The total is cut at 0x7ffff000, as one read's count is, and the buffer
that crosses it is filled only up to it.
The kernel copies the list once, as Linux does, and checks every length
first, then that every range lies in user space; the copy serves the whole
call, so a buffer that overwrites a later entry of the caller's list does
not move that entry's destination.
A destination that cannot be written is found by the copy.
A file, a device or a
.Pa /proc
file is read up to the first page that cannot be written, and the bytes
before it are returned, as Linux's read stops at the first fault of its
copy; when the first destination cannot be written the call gives
.Er EFAULT ,
but for a file of a mounted file system only after the check of the
list's end at the offset and when the file has a byte to read at the
offset; when it has none the call returns zero, as on Linux.
A pipe and a stream socket answer the wait, the end of input and
.Er EAGAIN
before any copy, so a list whose first buffer cannot be written reads zero
from an ended pipe.
A pipe then copies each of its page buffers whole, or as much of one as
the list takes, or not at all: a page that cannot be written ends the call
with the bytes of the buffers before it, or
.Er EFAULT ,
and every byte of its own buffer stays queued, as Linux's
.Fn pipe_read
leaves it.
A stream socket takes its bytes one receive buffer of the connection at a
time, as
.Xr recv 2
describes: a buffer whose destination cannot be written, or lies outside
user space, stays queued whole and ends the call with the bytes of the
buffers before it, or
.Er EFAULT .
A datagram socket gives
.Er EFAULT
when any copy of the datagram fails, and the datagram is consumed.
The descriptor is checked before the list, as for
.Fn read ,
so a list requesting no bytes returns zero only from a descriptor that
exists and is open for reading on an object with a read method, a
directory among them.
.Pp
The first nonempty buffer uses an ordinary read, which may wait.
For a regular file, reading continues through successive buffers until
a short read or error, with the open file description held across the
buffers, as Linux holds its position lock, so that a seek through a
descriptor that shares it comes before the call or after it, never
between two buffers; the end of the whole list at the offset is checked
under that hold.
A page of an ext2 file that a mapping holds in memory is read from that
page, as Linux reads its page cache, so a read sees a shared mapping's
stores before they are written back, and a buffer sees what an earlier
buffer of the same call stored into a mapping of the file.
A pipe and a stream socket are read as one read of the whole list, which
waits only for its first byte and takes what is queued, as much as the
buffers hold.
For a device or a
.Pa /proc
file, later buffers are read only while the descriptor reports read
readiness; a device that reports it immediately, a disk among them, can
still sleep in the read itself.
A terminal is read only once, into the first nonempty buffer.
.Pp
For a datagram, raw or packet socket, exactly one message is taken into a
kernel buffer before it is scattered across the destinations of the copied
list.
Bytes beyond the combined capacity are discarded.
A failed copy gives
.Er EFAULT ,
and the consumed message is not restored.
.Pp
Musl's buffered input uses
.Fn readv
and handles short returns by reading again.
.Ss Positioned reads
.Fn pread
reads up to
.Fa count
bytes at
.Fa offset
of the file
.Fa fd
is open on and leaves the descriptor's offset where it was.
The checks come in Linux's order.
A negative offset returns
.Er EINVAL
before the descriptor is looked at.
A pipe, a socket, a terminal, the mouse and a device that
.Xr lseek 2
refuses return
.Er ESPIPE ;
.Pa /dev/random
and
.Pa /dev/urandom
are among them, which Linux reads.
A descriptor not open for reading returns
.Er EBADF ,
a buffer range past the top of user memory
.Er EFAULT ,
a count negative as the kernel's signed size or an end past the signed 64-bit range
.Er EINVAL ,
and a directory
.Er EISDIR .
The end check passes
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /mem
and
.Pa /dev/mem ,
whose offsets are unsigned.
The range, count and end checks use the original request before
.Dv MAX_RW_COUNT
limits the transfer, including for a count of zero.
.Pp
A regular file, a raw disk window,
.Pa /dev/bootlog
and a file under
.Pa /proc
are read at
.Fa offset ;
.Pa /dev/null
and
.Pa /dev/zero
read as they always do.
The offset the descriptor shares with its duplicates does not change at
any point of the call, so a process that shares it can read, write or
seek meanwhile.
A zero count returns zero once the descriptor has passed these checks.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 3 for
.Nm read ,
145 for
.Nm readv ,
180 for
.Nm pread64 ,
which takes the descriptor, the buffer and the count in EBX, ECX and
EDX, and the offset's low and high 32 bits in ESI and EDI,
333 for
.Nm preadv ,
whose offset is in the fourth and fifth arguments, low word first, and
378 for
.Nm preadv2 ,
which adds flags in the sixth.
Musl's and GNU libc's
.Fn pread
are
.Nm pread64 .
.Pp
On x86-64 the numbers are 0 for
.Nm read ,
19 for
.Nm readv ,
17 for
.Nm pread64 ,
which takes the offset whole in R10, 295 for
.Nm preadv
and 327 for
.Nm preadv2 ,
each with the offset whole in its fourth argument and its fifth, the
offset's high half, not read, as on Linux.
.Fn preadv
reads the buffers of
.Fa iov
as
.Fn pread
reads one, each at the place the ones before it reached, from
.Fa offset ,
and leaves the descriptor's offset where it was; the offset and the
descriptor are checked first, as
.Fn pread
checks them, then the vector, as
.Fn readv
checks it, and a short read ends the call.
A file of a mounted file system is read under one hold of the open file
description, as
.Fn readv
reads it.
.Pp
The x86-64 kernel runs, for a program that enters it by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
which
.Xr ia32 7
describes, i386's
.Nm readv
(145), whose vector is of 32-bit addresses and lengths, and i386's
.Nm preadv
(333) and
.Nm preadv2
(378), with i386's arguments.
.Pp
.Nm preadv2
reads as
.Nm readv
at offset -1 and as
.Nm preadv
at another.
It checks the descriptor before the vector and flags, as Linux does: a descriptor
that is not open is
.Er EBADF
whatever the flags.
It then imports the vector, returning
.Er EFAULT
for an unreadable list and
.Er EINVAL
for an excessive count or a negative signed length.
A vector of no bytes returns zero before flags are checked.
The data pages are touched only after the flags have been accepted.
Both forms take
.Dv RWF_HIPRI ,
.Dv RWF_DSYNC ,
.Dv RWF_SYNC ,
.Dv RWF_APPEND
and
.Dv RWF_NOAPPEND ,
which a read ignores but on an
.Dv O_APPEND
descriptor of an append-only file, where it is
.Er EPERM ,
and refuse another flag with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
Combining
.Dv RWF_APPEND
and
.Dv RWF_NOAPPEND
is
.Er EINVAL
for a nonempty vector.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success, these functions return the number of bytes read.
Zero denotes end of a file or stream, a zero-length request, an empty
datagram, or a device's empty result.
.Fn readv
returns the total across the buffers; an error after a positive partial
transfer returns that total.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/readvmusl.c
reads a file containing
.Ql AAAABBBBCCCC
into three four-byte buffers with
.Fn readv .
It requires a return of 12 and the contents
.Ql AAAA ,
.Ql BBBB ,
and
.Ql CCCC
in that order.
It also checks pipe and datagram reads, that an invalid later buffer
leaves a pipe's input queued and fails a datagram with
.Er EFAULT ,
and that a datagram written over the caller's list does not move a later
destination.
.Pa tests/musl/preadmusl.c
reads a file at offsets with
.Fn pread
and checks that the descriptor's offset does not move, and that a pipe,
a socket and a terminal return
.Er ESPIPE .
It reads 2, 0 and 4 bytes from offset 13 with
.Fn preadv
and requires 6 and the bytes in turn.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ECONNREFUSED
.It Er EAGAIN
A nonblocking input would have to wait.
.It Er EBADF
The descriptor is invalid, is not open for reading, is of a kind with no
read, or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Er ECONNREFUSED
A stream connection was refused.
.It Er ECONNRESET
A stream connection was reset, or a closed stream with no buffered data,
no received FIN and no recorded error was read.
.It Er EFAULT
The buffer or vector list cannot be accessed as required, or there is
no current process.
.It Er EFBIG
An ext2 read requires a block beyond the file system's addressing limit.
.It Er EINTR
A signal interrupted a wait, or a background terminal read posted
.Dv SIGTTIN .
.It Er EINVAL
The vector count is outside 0 through 1024, a vector length is negative
as a signed size, a native i686 read or pread count is negative as the kernel's
signed size after its address range passes, a file system has no read
operation, the object the
descriptor is open on, such as an epoll set, has no read method, as on
Linux, or
.Fn pread
was given a negative offset.
A read at the description's offset or at that of
.Fn pread
would end past the signed 64-bit range, computed with the original count
before the transfer is limited, on a file other than
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /mem
and
.Pa /dev/mem .
A boot window,
.Pa /dev/bootpart
or
.Pa /dev/grubcore ,
also returns this error when the offset or count is not a multiple of
512 bytes; disk and partition nodes take any byte range.
An eventfd or a timerfd returns it for a read of fewer than eight bytes,
zero among them.
.It Er EIO
A file system or device read failed, a device was removed, or the
random device has no usable seed.
A background terminal read also gives this error when
.Dv SIGTTIN
is blocked or ignored, or its process group is orphaned.
.It Er EISDIR
The descriptor is open on a directory, as on Linux, whose entries
.Xr getdents64 2
reads.
.Fn read
and
.Fn pread
give it for a count of zero too;
.Fn readv
and
.Fn preadv
give it only for a vector with bytes, and return zero for one without.
.It Er ENETUNREACH
A stream connection timed out while its local address was gone.
.It Er ENOMEM
A page of the file that a mapping holds in memory could not be given a
temporary kernel mapping, which on i686 has 1024 slots; the bytes of the
pages before it are returned instead when there are any.
.It Er ENOTCONN
The stream socket is not connected and has not been shut down.
.It Er ENXIO
A boot window is not present; a removed disk's node gives
.Er EIO .
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
The descriptor is an ioctl-only socket, or
.Nm preadv2
was given a flag it does not take.
.It Er EPERM
A loop device node was read directly, or
.Fn preadv2
was given
.Dv RWF_NOAPPEND
on an
.Dv O_APPEND
descriptor of an append-only file.
.It Er ESPIPE
.Fn pread
was given a descriptor that cannot seek.
.It Er ETIMEDOUT
A stream connection timed out.
.El
.Pp
A raw disk read also propagates the block device's error when no sector
has yet been returned.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr getdents64 2 ,
.Xr lseek 2 ,
.Xr pipe 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr recv 2 ,
.Xr write 2
